  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1:       Percentage of smallholder farmers demonstrating knowledge to plan for and manage climate risk to support 
                       resilient agricultural production dis aggregated by gender

Output 2:        Number adopting new agricultural practices and alternative livelihoods 

Output 3:        Percentage of resilient commodities produced by target farmers that are sold on the markets</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2018">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1: TBA

Output 2: TBA

Output 3. TBA</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1. 90% Smallholder Farmers are able to plan for and manage water resources to support resilient agricultural production.

output 2: 10,000 small scale farmers adopting new agricultural practices and alternative livelihood; 16,000 farmers Increased access to agricultural inputs (e.g. seeds, soil kits, tools) for resilient crops; 30,000 farmers Introduced of new resilient agricultural production practices to strengthen production and diversify crops amidst climate variability and change; 7.055 farmers Introduced to alternative livelihoods to strengthen resilience in target communities:; Establish 80 farmer field schools and learning centres of excellence to further document and scale up successful practices.

Output 3: Increasing access to markets and commercialization of resilient agricultural products for 90,615 farmers; Strengthen processing of resilient products targeting 5 technologies; Strengthen storage, aggregation and transportation of resilient products to enhance commercialization and linkages to market and SMEs of 30,000 farmers;</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Farmers in 16 districts have enhanced use weather and agricultural advisories for risk informed livelihoods planning and implementation through regular daily and seasonal forecasts.
2. At least 1600 farmers participating in alternative livelihoods i.e. goat rearing and bee keeping; at least 80,000 farmers have accessed farming inputs under co financing through FISP; at least 40 farmer field schools have been established for enhanced farmer experiential learning in Nyimba and Mambwe districts.
3. Two centres of excellency have been established; 262 CEOs acquired post-harvest loss solutions; 3,551 trained on improved PHL solutions and commodity bulking centers identified in 3 districts; 262 key government staff trained on the agricultural commodity aggregation model, agricultural marketing and the Warehouse Receipt System from 14 of the 16 targeted districts.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Strengthen generation and interpretation of climate information and data collection to ensure timely and detailed weather, climate, crop and hydrological forecast to small scale farmers by installing 20 automatic weather station and 500 rain gauges in 220 agricultural camps.

2. Introduce 70,000 small scale farmers to new agricultural practices and 5,000 to alternative livelihood; 16,000 farmers Increased access to agricultural inputs (e.g. seeds, soil kits, tools) for resilient crops; Establish 100 farmer field schools and learning centres of excellence to further document and scale up successful practices.

3. Scale up provision of agricultural credit training and awareness raising on financial education programmes for farmers.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Farmers in 16 districts have enhanced use weather and agricultural advisories for risk informed livelihoods planning and implementation through regular daily and seasonal forecasts.

2. At least 4900 farmers participating in alternative livelihoods i.e. goat rearing and bee keeping; at least 80,000 farmers have accessed farming inputs under co financing through FISP; 70,000 farmers practicing conservation agriculture practices, experiential learning from the established 100 farmer field schools.

3. 14,000 farmers introduced to financial literacy and savings group for change; 320 savings group introduced to formal financial institutions;</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Strengthen the interpretation and dissemination of climate information at camp level through training of 150 agricultural camp extension officers in Agro- meteorology.

2. Construction of 10 solar powered drip irrigation schemes to increase access to water for agriculture and livestock production 550 the small scale farmers.

3. Construction of 6 aggregation centres for grain bulking to increase access to the market for small scale farmers and link 20 aggregators to financial service providers.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
   </indicator>
